1
00:00:15,808 --> 00:00:17,108
So what are we doing tomorrow?

2
00:00:17,978 --> 00:00:19,108
Tomorrow,

3
00:00:19,748 --> 00:00:21,468
- we'll start work at dawn.
- At what time?

4
00:00:21,948 --> 00:00:23,847
We'll take off at 5:30am.

5
00:00:23,848 --> 00:00:24,848
Right.

6
00:00:25,248 --> 00:00:26,388
Taking off at 5:30am?

7
00:00:26,888 --> 00:00:27,958
Oh, my gosh.

8
00:00:29,558 --> 00:00:30,687
At 5:30am?

9
00:00:30,688 --> 00:00:32,128
(Surprised at the very early time)

10
00:00:32,528 --> 00:00:34,028
To take off at 5:30am,

11
00:00:34,598 --> 00:00:36,098
we'll have to get up at 4:50am.

12
00:00:37,368 --> 00:00:38,427
Oh, my goodness.

13
00:00:38,428 --> 00:00:39,644
(It's their first time working at the crack of dawn.)

14
00:00:39,668 --> 00:00:40,697
Oh, my goodness.

15
00:00:40,698 --> 00:00:42,138
(They're already worried.)

16
00:00:42,638 --> 00:00:44,708
(An early hour when the sun is just rising)

17
00:00:45,838 --> 00:00:48,278
(What is waiting for them in the day ahead?)

18
00:00:48,778 --> 00:00:51,408
I'm going to wash up and go to bed. I'm so tired.

19
00:00:52,778 --> 00:00:53,818
Oh, goodness.

20
00:00:56,288 --> 00:00:58,418
Right. We should turn this off.

21
00:01:00,488 --> 00:01:01,918
- I'll set an alarm.
- What?

22
00:01:02,188 --> 00:01:03,358
I'll set an alarm.

23
00:01:03,528 --> 00:01:04,857
- But your alarm...
- You'll be woken up...

24
00:01:04,858 --> 00:01:06,458
by my alarm anyway.

25
00:01:06,598 --> 00:01:07,898
Your alarm will wake me up.

26
00:01:08,158 --> 00:01:11,067
We have to wake up at 4:30am.

27
00:01:11,068 --> 00:01:12,398
(Crying)

28
00:01:12,968 --> 00:01:14,497
Sleep well, You Seok.

29
00:01:14,498 --> 00:01:15,498
Sleep well.

30
00:01:15,938 --> 00:01:17,038
Sleep tight.

31
00:01:19,308 --> 00:01:23,448
(Goodnight, everyone.)

32
00:01:24,648 --> 00:01:27,378
(The moon lights up the night sky.)

33
00:01:28,418 --> 00:01:31,348
(Maegye Village is deep asleep.)

34
00:01:32,148 --> 00:01:37,258
(The blue light of dawn slowly starts to appear.)

35
00:01:38,188 --> 00:01:40,328
(Current time: 4:30am)

36
00:01:41,228 --> 00:01:44,268
(The siblings aren't moving at all in the darkness.)

37
00:01:47,938 --> 00:01:51,268
(The sound of the alarm cuts through the silence.)

38
00:01:52,338 --> 00:01:55,708
(Yoon Seo got up first.)

39
00:01:57,108 --> 00:01:58,507
Do you want to wash up first, Yoon Seo?

40
00:01:58,508 --> 00:02:00,747
- Just my face. Just a minute.
- Okay.

41
00:02:00,748 --> 00:02:02,378
(I'll go first and wash my face.)

42
00:02:03,018 --> 00:02:05,548
(Having a hard time waking up so early in the morning)

43
00:02:06,688 --> 00:02:08,988
We should stretch a bit, don't you think?

44
00:02:09,218 --> 00:02:10,487
My body feels so slow.

45
00:02:10,488 --> 00:02:12,827
Yoon Seo, you're so pretty right now. Stay still.

46
00:02:12,828 --> 00:02:14,157
What are you talking about?

47
00:02:14,158 --> 00:02:15,197
You can close your eyes.

48
00:02:15,198 --> 00:02:16,198
(Unsure what to do)

49
00:02:16,898 --> 00:02:20,168
(She has to get some pictures when Yoon Seo looks pretty.)

50
00:02:20,768 --> 00:02:24,138
(Our Yoon Seo is so pretty, even when she's half awake.)

51
00:02:25,138 --> 00:02:28,308
(Squirming around)

52
00:02:28,608 --> 00:02:30,078
Am I pretty right now?

53
00:02:30,508 --> 00:02:31,548
What?

54
00:02:32,348 --> 00:02:33,628
I'm pretty right now too, right?

55
00:02:35,478 --> 00:02:36,518
No?

56
00:02:36,848 --> 00:02:38,448
Not in particular...

57
00:02:39,318 --> 00:02:41,588
(Waiting expectantly)

58
00:02:41,918 --> 00:02:43,188
Your face is super puffy.

59
00:02:44,928 --> 00:02:47,428
(Even early in the morning, Jung Ah is firm.)

60
00:02:47,828 --> 00:02:49,628
Yoon Seo, how do I look right now?

61
00:02:50,068 --> 00:02:51,198
My beauty.

62
00:02:51,598 --> 00:02:53,038
So lovely.

63
00:02:53,698 --> 00:02:56,038
- You're the best, Yoon Seo.
- You look pretty.

64
00:02:56,968 --> 00:02:58,068
What time is it now?

65
00:02:58,708 --> 00:02:59,838
Let's see.

66
00:03:00,838 --> 00:03:03,578
It's 5:03am. You need to get ready.

67
00:03:04,548 --> 00:03:07,248
(Turning on the lights to really start getting ready)

68
00:03:08,218 --> 00:03:09,278
You Seok.

69
00:03:11,448 --> 00:03:13,918
(Sitting right up)

70
00:03:14,688 --> 00:03:17,928
(Now everyone is up, including You Seok.)

71
00:03:18,758 --> 00:03:20,628
I'll just wash my face here.

72
00:03:23,968 --> 00:03:25,297
(Sun Young starts washing her face at the outdoor faucet.)

73
00:03:25,298 --> 00:03:27,298
(Pretty thorough)

74
00:03:28,268 --> 00:03:31,508
(Washing vigorously)

75
00:03:32,368 --> 00:03:35,407
(Cleaning foam is a luxury.)

76
00:03:35,408 --> 00:03:37,648
(She just roughly washes her face with water.)

77
00:03:38,508 --> 00:03:41,718
(Wild Sun Young)

78
00:03:42,448 --> 00:03:45,348
(Pushing her hair back)

79
00:03:45,948 --> 00:03:47,017
There we go.

80
00:03:47,018 --> 00:03:48,018
(Refreshed)

81
00:03:48,488 --> 00:03:50,804
(Right as Sun Young finished her wild outdoor face cleanse, )

82
00:03:50,828 --> 00:03:52,787
(You Seok comes out of the bathroom.)

83
00:03:52,788 --> 00:03:53,857
How do I look, You Seok?

84
00:03:53,858 --> 00:03:55,427
My face right now. Do I look great?

85
00:03:55,428 --> 00:03:56,428
- Today?
- Yes.

86
00:03:56,429 --> 00:03:58,498
- A six on the die.
- Yes.

87
00:03:58,898 --> 00:04:00,198
What's "six on the die?"

88
00:04:01,698 --> 00:04:03,258
- You know it, right, Yoon Seo?
- What?

89
00:04:03,668 --> 00:04:06,037
When someone says, "You rolled a six today,"

90
00:04:06,038 --> 00:04:07,377
- Yes.
- it means you look very pretty.

91
00:04:07,378 --> 00:04:08,407
Okay, I got it.

92
00:04:08,408 --> 00:04:09,777
- When you roll die, - You rolled die,

93
00:04:09,778 --> 00:04:11,654
- six is the highest number.
- and you got a six.

94
00:04:11,678 --> 00:04:13,978
(My appearance is a six on die?)

95
00:04:14,478 --> 00:04:16,078
I rolled a six today.

96
00:04:16,748 --> 00:04:18,187
(Feeling good)

97
00:04:18,188 --> 00:04:20,718
Can you say, "D-Six?" too? Do people say that?

98
00:04:20,888 --> 00:04:22,818
- No, they just say six on die.
- Okay.

99
00:04:24,688 --> 00:04:25,887
She learned about shortening phrases,

100
00:04:25,888 --> 00:04:27,408
and now she tries to shorten anything.

101
00:04:28,128 --> 00:04:29,297
I got ready the quickest.

102
00:04:29,298 --> 00:04:30,618
(Starting with our happy D-Six, )

103
00:04:30,798 --> 00:04:35,238
(everyone is now ready to go to work.)

104
00:04:35,538 --> 00:04:36,938
- Shall we get going?
- Yes.

105
00:04:38,968 --> 00:04:42,708
(You Seok's choice for today's drive-to-work song)

106
00:04:43,548 --> 00:04:45,777
(From now)

107
00:04:45,778 --> 00:04:49,277
(You'll lose your smile, "Uphill" by Yoon Jong Shin)

108
00:04:49,278 --> 00:04:50,948
We're going uphill right now.

109
00:04:51,688 --> 00:04:58,158
(Look at this steep road)

110
00:04:58,228 --> 00:05:03,567
(I love you, the one going to work with me)

111
00:05:03,568 --> 00:05:06,198
(Wistful)

112
00:05:06,538 --> 00:05:08,967
(Worried)

113
00:05:08,968 --> 00:05:13,238
(You who chose tough work)

114
00:05:13,608 --> 00:05:16,337
What is in store for us today?

115
00:05:16,338 --> 00:05:19,918
(What sort of uphill road will they climb up today?)

116
00:05:20,848 --> 00:05:23,288
(Meanwhile)

117
00:05:23,648 --> 00:05:26,987
(This place has an oddly bleak air.)

118
00:05:26,988 --> 00:05:29,218
(Hazardous materials storage)

119
00:05:32,758 --> 00:05:35,698
(This is where the siblings are headed.)

120
00:05:36,258 --> 00:05:42,937
(This place is full of mysterious fixtures.)

121
00:05:42,938 --> 00:05:47,508
(A mysterious man appears.)

122
00:05:49,108 --> 00:05:53,718
(He suspiciously stands in front of the mirror.)

123
00:05:54,518 --> 00:05:56,618
(Sliding out his phone)

124
00:05:57,848 --> 00:05:58,888
(Snap)

125
00:06:05,428 --> 00:06:08,628
(Today's guest, Ji Jin Hee)

126
00:06:09,258 --> 00:06:13,398
(Jin Hee is a master before-work mirror selfies.)

127
00:06:13,668 --> 00:06:14,914
Since I took one, I should post it.

128
00:06:14,938 --> 00:06:16,938
(Since I took one, I should post it on Instagram!)

129
00:06:17,508 --> 00:06:20,377
(With his phone that opens up, )

130
00:06:20,378 --> 00:06:23,207
(he's doing his daily routine.)

131
00:06:23,208 --> 00:06:26,948
(Lips pursed in concentration)

132
00:06:30,388 --> 00:06:31,388
I posted it.

133
00:06:31,389 --> 00:06:33,588
What hashtag are you going to put?

134
00:06:34,488 --> 00:06:35,528
"On the way to a new job?"

135
00:06:36,928 --> 00:06:39,428
Oh, I should change it from "new" to "my other." Hold on.

136
00:06:44,698 --> 00:06:46,298
I don't know how to change it.

137
00:06:48,768 --> 00:06:49,837
I'll just post it as is.

138
00:06:49,838 --> 00:06:52,638
(Giving up without hesitation)

139
00:06:54,108 --> 00:06:55,228
This pine tree is beautiful.

140
00:06:56,108 --> 00:06:59,447
Yes, the trees with straight trunks and branches at the top.

141
00:06:59,448 --> 00:07:01,024
(Suddenly being impressed by pine trees)

142
00:07:01,048 --> 00:07:04,618
Apparently, these are the pine trees that are native to Korea.

143
00:07:04,718 --> 00:07:07,064
The ones with the straight trunks and the branches at the top.

144
00:07:07,088 --> 00:07:09,217
Their lumber makes good building materials.

145
00:07:09,218 --> 00:07:10,458
The bendy pine trees...

146
00:07:10,728 --> 00:07:13,257
It must be hard for them to grow so closely packed together.

147
00:07:13,258 --> 00:07:14,997
I think they need more space.

148
00:07:14,998 --> 00:07:16,798
(Does he have some connection to pine trees?)

149
00:07:16,998 --> 00:07:18,228
Oh, Mount Jiri...

150
00:07:21,098 --> 00:07:24,208
Just breathing the air here feels healing.

151
00:07:25,038 --> 00:07:27,808
What are you looking forward to today?

152
00:07:28,508 --> 00:07:30,078
Jung Ah is such...

153
00:07:30,878 --> 00:07:32,478
a good friend of mine.

154
00:07:33,148 --> 00:07:36,247
I'm always happy to see Jung Ah.

155
00:07:36,248 --> 00:07:37,624
(He came on the show to spend time with Jung Ah.)

156
00:07:37,648 --> 00:07:39,088
How long have you known each other?

157
00:07:39,688 --> 00:07:41,518
It's been over 20 years.

158
00:07:41,958 --> 00:07:43,528
My first movie...

159
00:07:44,558 --> 00:07:46,257
I filmed it with her.

160
00:07:46,258 --> 00:07:48,628
We filmed another movie together after that as well.

161
00:07:49,328 --> 00:07:51,228
Also, I was the MC...

162
00:07:51,428 --> 00:07:53,198
at Jung Ah's wedding.

163
00:07:53,768 --> 00:07:55,537
We always see each other...

164
00:07:55,538 --> 00:07:58,368
at big events like weddings and funerals.

165
00:07:59,868 --> 00:08:02,149
(He took a seat to share more information about Jung Ah.)

166
00:08:02,478 --> 00:08:04,648
We were in Busan together for over seven months.

167
00:08:05,308 --> 00:08:06,408
For 15 days...

168
00:08:07,518 --> 00:08:10,587
One time, we had to wait 15 days to shoot our next scene.

169
00:08:10,588 --> 00:08:12,018
(For some reason)

170
00:08:12,388 --> 00:08:13,858
Jung Ah was in the next room.

171
00:08:15,118 --> 00:08:18,327
You know what she was always doing? She was cleaning.

172
00:08:18,328 --> 00:08:20,598
(She was cleaning!)

173
00:08:20,858 --> 00:08:23,698
I mean, that's fine, but she cleans again the next day.

174
00:08:24,868 --> 00:08:26,198
And the next day, and the next.

175
00:08:26,838 --> 00:08:29,698
The day after that, she was washing all of the curtains.

176
00:08:30,338 --> 00:08:33,838
Then, she flipped over all of the sofas to clean them.

177
00:08:34,978 --> 00:08:37,048
She kept cleaning, saying it was nice to be clean.

178
00:08:37,608 --> 00:08:39,348
She cleaned for 15 days.

179
00:08:39,708 --> 00:08:40,847
(She's still the same 20 years later...)

180
00:08:40,848 --> 00:08:42,747
I thought she was interesting.

181
00:08:42,748 --> 00:08:44,268
(She's still the same 20 years later.)

182
00:08:44,818 --> 00:08:45,887
(How will Jin Hee react when he meets the siblings?)

183
00:08:45,888 --> 00:08:47,357
- Are they on their way?
- Pardon?

184
00:08:47,358 --> 00:08:48,418
Are they on their way?

185
00:08:48,988 --> 00:08:51,528
- We're going to Gwangyang.
- What is there?

186
00:08:52,328 --> 00:08:56,328
Let's see. What's waiting for us 400m ahead?

187
00:08:57,468 --> 00:08:59,068
- Is it field work?
- Is it field work?

188
00:08:59,598 --> 00:09:00,737
(All they see outside of the window are green fields...)

189
00:09:00,738 --> 00:09:02,098
I don't think it'll be field work.

190
00:09:02,298 --> 00:09:04,407
They wouldn't send us all the way here for field work.

191
00:09:04,408 --> 00:09:05,938
There are many fields back there too.

192
00:09:06,238 --> 00:09:07,477
- Eel...
- But we did go one hour...

193
00:09:07,478 --> 00:09:10,307
- to Geoje to harvest onions.
- In a moment, turn right.

194
00:09:10,308 --> 00:09:11,807
Jung Ah, we did go.

195
00:09:11,808 --> 00:09:13,747
(Jung Ah, they do send us out 1 hour for field work.)

196
00:09:13,748 --> 00:09:14,878
We went for one hour.

197
00:09:15,748 --> 00:09:16,748
Here?

198
00:09:16,749 --> 00:09:19,269
(While they're all confused about what work they'll do today, )

199
00:09:20,258 --> 00:09:22,518
(they drive into a spooky forest road.)

200
00:09:23,428 --> 00:09:25,488
What could it be? I'm curious.

201
00:09:26,358 --> 00:09:27,957
They're all wearing rain boots.

202
00:09:27,958 --> 00:09:29,658
They're going to make us wear those.

203
00:09:30,398 --> 00:09:31,767
They told us to wear these pants...

204
00:09:31,768 --> 00:09:34,208
- so we can wear those boots.
- The boots that come up there.

205
00:09:34,498 --> 00:09:36,868
- Thank you for driving.
- Have a good day.

206
00:09:37,068 --> 00:09:40,908
We have to wear those rain boots.

207
00:09:42,078 --> 00:09:43,138
It's eel.

208
00:09:44,048 --> 00:09:45,077
Why are you hiding it?

209
00:09:45,078 --> 00:09:47,377
Shall we go catch some eel?

210
00:09:47,378 --> 00:09:48,977
- Shall we?
- Let's go.

211
00:09:48,978 --> 00:09:50,018
(Will it really be eel?)

212
00:09:50,788 --> 00:09:55,088
(The siblings follow the road.)

213
00:09:55,418 --> 00:09:56,618
What could be here?

214
00:09:56,728 --> 00:10:00,058
- There must be an eel farm there.
- Really?

215
00:10:00,328 --> 00:10:03,768
(This is an eel farm?)

216
00:10:03,898 --> 00:10:06,198
- They farm eels here.
- It has to be eel.

217
00:10:06,468 --> 00:10:08,638
It has to be eel. Right?

218
00:10:08,738 --> 00:10:09,868
Oh, but eels are...

219
00:10:10,268 --> 00:10:12,737
This is a farm. They pull the water in from here.

220
00:10:12,738 --> 00:10:13,777
Oh, my. This is going to be tough.

221
00:10:13,778 --> 00:10:17,178
I really love eel, but an eel farm...

222
00:10:17,948 --> 00:10:19,378
It must be hard to catch eels.

223
00:10:20,118 --> 00:10:21,718
- So...
- Hey.

224
00:10:21,748 --> 00:10:24,987
We have something to tell you about today's labor.

225
00:10:24,988 --> 00:10:26,017
Okay.

226
00:10:26,018 --> 00:10:29,357
There are instructions and precautions on the mirror.

227
00:10:29,358 --> 00:10:30,527
Please go and take a look.

228
00:10:30,528 --> 00:10:33,328
(Instructions?)

229
00:10:34,058 --> 00:10:35,958
We have to catch eels with our bare hands?

230
00:10:36,898 --> 00:10:38,538
- "Bare hands, bare feet..."
- Bare feet?

231
00:10:39,168 --> 00:10:42,398
(On one side of the mirror)

232
00:10:42,738 --> 00:10:45,167
(Safety Precautions, 1. You must work barehanded.)

233
00:10:45,168 --> 00:10:47,177
(2. You must work barefooted.)

234
00:10:47,178 --> 00:10:49,178
(Shocked)

235
00:10:49,438 --> 00:10:51,748
- "You must work barehanded."
- Two.

236
00:10:52,348 --> 00:10:54,347
- "You must work barefooted."
- Three.

237
00:10:54,348 --> 00:10:57,147
"Keep a safe distance from each other."

238
00:10:57,148 --> 00:10:59,587
- Four.
- "You cannot leave in the middle."

239
00:10:59,588 --> 00:11:01,018
That sounds scary.

240
00:11:01,518 --> 00:11:03,558
- Oh, my goodness!
- Oh, my!

241
00:11:04,988 --> 00:11:08,328
(Oh, my goodness!)

242
00:11:08,798 --> 00:11:12,027
(What in the world...)

243
00:11:12,028 --> 00:11:14,297
- What was that?
- That really scared me.

244
00:11:14,298 --> 00:11:15,638
(What in the world did they see?)

245
00:11:15,838 --> 00:11:16,838
That's so scary.

246
00:11:16,938 --> 00:11:18,838
- Oh, my goodness!
- Oh, my!

247
00:11:19,768 --> 00:11:23,138
(Why is Ji Jin Hee in there?)

248
00:11:24,148 --> 00:11:26,348
(30 minutes before the siblings arrived)

249
00:11:27,018 --> 00:11:32,488
(The mirror he took a selfie in front of had a secret.)

250
00:11:33,148 --> 00:11:36,058
Real mirrors, when you put your hand on them,

251
00:11:36,558 --> 00:11:38,087
have some space between your hand and the reflection.

252
00:11:38,088 --> 00:11:40,688
But with fake mirrors, your hand directly touches the reflection.

253
00:11:40,958 --> 00:11:44,098
This is a fake mirror. It's a two-way mirror.

254
00:11:44,398 --> 00:11:46,498
(At a glance, it looks like a normal mirror.)

255
00:11:47,198 --> 00:11:48,367
This is well made.

256
00:11:48,368 --> 00:11:49,867
(There's space behind the mirror.)

257
00:11:49,868 --> 00:11:51,838
(Cozy)

258
00:11:53,068 --> 00:11:55,508
(When the light is turned on, you can see inside.)

259
00:11:56,178 --> 00:11:57,247
This will be fun.

260
00:11:57,248 --> 00:11:58,248
